  blocks so she wouldn't feel the grief  over being abandoned by her father when  she finally makes peace with both her  father's abandonment and the fact that  xavier z' mental manipulation was a bad  decision made for the right reasons of  love and protection she's finally able  to take full control of the Phoenix  force and end the alien threat while  Jean Grey seems like she's the main  protagonist of the film she's not the  only character to go on a journey and  grow as a person  Professor Xavier likewise has an arc  that sees him change from when the film  starts to when it ends he begins the  story in a place of power and certainty  in his correctness whenever he issues an  order it's the right call no matter the  cost that others have to bear he's proud  and confident and as the film continues  when he learned that he's also pretty  stubborn in the face of evidence that  sometimes he makes the wrong call sure  Paul Schultz  the turning point for Xavier comes when  he and the rest of the x-men are trapped  in that prison train wending its way  through upstate New York he admits he  was wrong to place mental blocks in  genes mind meddling with her memories  and her ability to process her trauma  and apologizes to beast and everyone  else for his role in genes descent into  a parent super villainy Xavier figures  out that while it's great to take credit  for the good things you do in life you  can't use that to cover up the bad  things you might do as well as a wise  woman once told us you take the good you  take the bad you take them both and  there you have the facts of life the  facts of life  the final scene in the film shows  lifelong friend amis Professor X and  Magneto settling in to play a game of  chess at a public cafe somewhere in  France the two seem to be on good terms  and not currently sitting in prison  cells despite the fact that while the  climax of the movie built up the US  government had plans to establish mutant  internment camps we never 